The 3GPP protocol specifications, kept at the root rather than under a
package because they are not one package's fixtures: they are whole protocols,
and more than the parser will want to read them.

3gpp/
    13 protocol specifications, taken from the 3GPP specification archive on
    2026-09-24 by tools/corpus/extract.py, which downloads each one and lifts
    the ASN.1 out of the Word document it is written in. Run that script to
    refresh them; it names the specifications it reads and the ones it cannot.

    Licence: these are 3GPP's own technical specifications, which 3GPP
    publishes for the purpose of implementing them. They are taken from the
    source rather than from another project that had already copied them, so
    that what they are and where they came from is not in question.

    They are here because they are the largest ASN.1 anyone writes and were
    not written with any parser in mind. The radio resource protocols carry
    constructed types and extension markers at a size no hand-written module
    reaches; the application protocols are information object classes almost
    end to end, a class per message and an object set per message's contents.
    Between them they found two defects the asn1c corpus did not.
